ollows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{4}{x+3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{x+3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x+3} & =1 \\\\ 4 & =x+3 \\\\ 1 & =x \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-2,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=1$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}(1-\\frac{4}{x+3})\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139545755 17592186576411 false] [17592186398438 1074 "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=1-\\frac{4}{x+3}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{4}{x+3} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4}{x+3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{x+3} & =1 \\\\ 4 & =x+3 \\\\ 1 & =x \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-2,5],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=5.$\n\nSo, $a=1$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\left(1-\\frac{4}{x+3}\\right)\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\left(1-\\frac{4}{x+3}\\right)\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139545755 17592186576411 true] [17592186398500 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=2x,\\,y=5x,\\,y=\\frac{3}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=2,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5,\\,xy=3,\\,xy=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v(x,y)=xy,$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to (\\,\\frac{y}{x}\\,,\\,xy).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v &=xy.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}$ back into $y=ux,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=u\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}=\\sqrt{uv}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $T$ can be given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}},\\,\\sqrt{uv}).\n$$" 13194139545755 17592186576411 false] [17592186398500 1074 "We would like to find a rectangular region of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v\\le d} \\\\  & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,a\\le u(x,y)\\le b,\\,\\,c\\le v(x,y)\\le d},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the equations of the left, right, bottom, and top sides are\n\n$$\nu(x,y)=a,\\,u(x,y)=b,\\,v(x,y)=c,\\,v(x,y)=d.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze the boundary of our region $D.$ \n\nRewriting the equations \n\n$$\ny=2x,\\,y=5x,\\,y=\\frac{3}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x}\n$$\n\nby moving both variables $x$ and $y$ to the left-hand side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=2,\\,\\frac{y}{x}=5,\\,xy=3,\\,xy=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if we put $u(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$ and $v(x,y)=xy,$ we can define the transformation ${T}^{-1}$ as\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:(x,y)\\to \\left(\\,\\frac{y}{x}\\,,\\,xy\\right).\n$$\n\nTo find $T,$ we should express $x$ and $y$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$ Recall that we have\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ v &=xy.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $y=ux.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\nv=u{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}=\\frac{v}{u}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{v}{u}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=\