tion\n\n$$\n2x+2y=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{2}-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{15}{2}-x$ in the expression $A=xy$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =x\\left(\\frac{15}{2}-x\\right) \\\\ A & =\\frac{15}{2}x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${A}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=\\frac{15}{2}-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{15}{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(x)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=\\frac{15}{4}$, we have \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{15}{2}-\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=\\frac{15}{4}$ and $y=\\frac{15}{4},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nA=xy=\\frac{15}{4}\\cdot \\frac{15}{4}≈14.06\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139545755 17592186576411 true] [17592186399468 1074 "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y &=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\en