)" 13194139545755 17592186576411 true] [17592186403710 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\langle \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1\\right\\rangle \n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}-3,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 &=0 \\\\ 2y+2 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\pm 1$ while the second equation gives $y=-1.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-1)^{3}-3(-1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(1)^{3}-3(1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(1,-1,-3).$" 13194139545755 17592186576411 true] [17592186403710 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}-3,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 &=0 \\\\ 2y+2 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\pm 1$ while the second equation gives $y=-1.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-1)^{3}-3(-1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(1)^{3}-3(1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(1,-1,-3).$" 13194139545755 17592186576411 false] [17592186403724 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\langle \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1\\right\\rangle \n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,4,2⟩.$ So, the no