c{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139545755 17592186576411 false] [17592186411860 1074 "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/eb/eb8745d968da24a8500bd079638b17bc461ab2d97b93d1d1a7d6d4834365bb5a.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is give