ticle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{49}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{v}_{0}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=1$ to $x=4$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-4=3\\,m/s.\n$$" 13194139545755 17592186576411 false] [17592186416266 1074 "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\left(3-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left[3x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\left(12+\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-(3+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{4}\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{33}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\fra